
Designing My Google Blog

 Big Plans: Making it Look Like Netflix

The Blogger card buttons were so big that only two or three could fit on the screen, which really bugged me. I tried tweaking the themes here and there, but I just couldn't get the look I had in mind.

I envisioned something like Netflix—clean card buttons that featured just an image, a title, and maybe a small label. I wanted those near-square cards to scroll horizontally. But no matter what I tried, I couldn’t get the image to sit perfectly inside the card like a framed picture. Eventually, I gave up.

In the end, I stripped out the body text preview from the cards, kept it minimal with just the label and title, and finally got them to scroll sideways. Spending two whole days adjusting the code bit by bit and checking the live preview felt endless.

However, I managed to use HTML editing to inject a custom gadget into the body, linking it to Soosooland's games. It turned out so sleek that I completely forgot about the plain-looking cards at the top.

I put so much time into fixing up Blogger. Since I’m fickle and get bored easily, I knew that if I kept tweaking until I was 100% satisfied, it would never end. It wasn't perfect, but I figured it had its own unique charm, so I promised myself to just live with it for a month.

---

The "Add a Gadget" Trick I Learned Later

Look for "Add a Gadget" under the Layout tab. Depending on your theme, there may or may not be an option to add it directly to the body area. If it’s missing from the body, you have to add it to the sidebar menu first, then click and drag it over to the body area. 


It was a real pain to drag and drop; it just wouldn't follow my mouse smoothly, taking quite a few frustrating attempts before it finally snapped into place.


Alternatively, you could add it directly via HTML, but the code varies wildly by theme, making it a massive hassle.

---

A Blog with a Korean-English Toggle

I realized that putting both English and Korean text into a single post would make the scrolling painfully long. So, I started publishing them separately, adding a `[Read in Korean]` button to the English posts and a `[Read in English]` button to the Korean ones.

Every single post followed this rule until one day, I asked Claude:

> *"Claude, is it possible to add a language toggle button on Blogger?"*

Claude immediately showed me how. Moving forward, I’ll be using this toggle button. But for the older posts, I went back and removed the manual language buttons. Since English posts have English titles and Korean posts have Korean titles, the buttons felt redundant anyway. Plus, with the credit at the bottom (*"Written by Han Soosoo, translated by Claude"*) and the Soosooland button already there, adding another language switcher just felt too cluttered.

---

Giving the Blog a Facelift

I told myself I’d put up with it for a month, but three months flew by.

The horizontal scroll I set up back in April was supposed to give off Netflix vibes, but it lacked the width, and without images, the text-only cards just felt empty. On top of that, while the "Read More" link worked, there was no "Back" button at all. It was time for a change.

When I consulted Claude, it suggested adding a gadget to the body and inserting a new snippet of code. Goodbye horizontal scroll, hello vertical layout.

I cleared out the default "View Posts" and "Popular Posts" sections from the body. Then, I added labels to the cards and completely hid the body text preview, making the entire dashboard look much cleaner.

