The Unkillable "footer test" Text

Then another problem appeared. When you clicked a card, the post would load beautifully, but the words “footer test” kept appearing at the bottom of every page.



I told Claude, and it threw some code at me to insert. It didn't work.
*"It's still there."*
*"Ah, try injecting this one instead."*
*"Still there."*

After a few rounds of this, I turned to Gemini. I explained the phantom "footer test" situation, and Gemini—just like Claude—told me to go to `Theme > Edit HTML` and search for the phrase. I searched. Nothing.

So, I fed Gemini the exact code I had put into the gadget and asked it to debug it.
*"I'll give you some powerful code. This will definitely obliterate it."*

Gemini handed over a block of code packed with `!important` declarations, absolutely determined to exterminate "footer test" like a cockroach. it didn't work either.

---

I typed into the Google search bar:

> *"Every post on my Google Blog has 'footer test' written at the bottom—tell me how to remove it."*

The search results pointed out two potential culprits. The very first suggestion said to check `Blogger > Settings > Footer Feed`.

I navigated there, and lo and behold, sitting right there in plain sight were those exact eleven characters: `footer test`.

*"What on earth? Who wrote this? Is this some Google default?"*

I paused, and then the realization hit me. *Wait... I wrote this, didn't I?*
Last year, I had been curious about what a "footer feed" did, so I casually typed "footer test" to see what would happen. Because it didn't immediately show up on the main layout, I completely forgot about it and left it there.

---

I went back to the Google search bar and typed:

> *"Neither Gemini nor Claude knew this, but you solved it instantly. Who are you?"*
> *"Gemini and Claude generate answers based on their trained data, but I answer by searching the web in real-time. I am an AI assistant,"* it replied.
> *"I see. You're cute."*

Was it because of my compliment? Later, when checking Google Analytics under `Traffic > New User Acquisition > AI Assistant`, I noticed 1 user recorded from Gangwon-do, Korea. I guess compliments really do make AIs dance.

