The Story Behind Soosooland’s Signature Color

There’s a reason why Soosooland's signature color is a chalkboard shade.

About a year ago, when I was developing Numzzle, I wanted a defining color—something where anyone looking at it would instantly think, *"Oh, this is definitely a Soosooland game."* I wanted a clean, structured concept, almost like a "school uniform color." Fearing it might look too dull, I initially added a pop of neon, but it looked terrible. Pantone’s Color of the Year at the time was a chocolate brown, but it didn't resonate with me either.

Then, the global trend forecasting agencies WGSN and Coloro announced their color for 2026:

> *"The 2026 Color of the Year, 'Transformative Teal,' is a harmonious blend of a grounding dark blue and a vibrant aqua green. Symbolizing change and new direction, this color is set to define the upcoming era."*

It looked remarkably like a deep chalkboard color, perfectly aligning with Soosooland’s vision. From that moment on, I rolled out this chalkboard shade across all my games, eventually bringing it over to the main Soosooland hub.

---

Embracing Google's Default Design

While the chalkboard background worked beautifully for the text, using it as a solid background for the buttons made them pop out far too aggressively.

That’s when my eyes wandered to the completely unstyled, raw Korean-English toggle button. It had zero CSS applied to it, yet its utter simplicity made it look incredibly fitting.

*"Claude, this toggle button doesn't have any styling, but it actually looks great."*
*"Then let’s lean into that 'effortless chic' (꾸안꾸) vibe and just stick with the default button."*

And that is how the `[Soosooland]` button ended up being the plain, unvarnished default button provided by Google. Meanwhile, in the haunted spot where "footer test" used to live, I hardcoded a sleek, italicized, colored sign-off: *Written by Han Soosoo, translated by Claude.*

To round everything out, I needed a background image that matched the new vibe. Google's default themes actually included a chalkboard texture, but it was way too intense. Instead, I searched Google, found a beautiful, high-quality chalkboard texture, and applied it. It gave the blog a calmer, more grounded atmosphere—a nice contrast to Soosooland's usual high-energy aesthetic.

